"# Flags for lib tools\n\nLib tools accept schema-derived flags as another way to supply their JSON input keys.\n\nFor example:\n\n```sh\naos-exec lib/base/read --path a.txt --max-lines 1\n```\n\nFlags override stdin keys. `--a.b V` changes only that leaf, keeping other keys under a. In a flag `-` stands for `_` in the key name (`--max-lines` = `max_lines`). A boolean key also takes `--no-KEY` for false. An array key takes its flag repeated, one item each time; the first one replaces the array from stdin. A tool definition may list `positional` keys: plain words then fill them in order. A top-level `description` key in stdin is tolerated. Unknown stdin keys give `ArgumentsBad`. Unknown flags give `FlagUnknown`. A terminal stdin is treated as `{}` without reading it. `--tooldef`, `--metainfo` and `--write-defs [FOLDER]` (lib tools only) print or write the tool's definition files instead of running it.\n\nParameters, defaults and declared error codes in `--help` come from `_tooldef.json`; prose explains the results. Human commands use `aos_flags`, which shares the tokenizer in `lib/tool_io.py`. This does not make human CLI flags into a stdin JSON object; stdin still carries content such as message text or tool arguments.\n"
